   "name": "aloe-vera.md",
   "text": "title: Aloe Vera\naka: Aloe barbadensis miller\ntags: plant medicinal herb\nwarn: Oral use carries more serious risks: the latex can cause abdominal pain and diarrhea, oral extracts have been linked to cases of acute hepatitis, and animal studies associated non-decolorized extracts with gastrointestinal cancer. Likely unsafe during pregnancy; may interact with medications such as digoxin.\nsource: https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/aloe-vera, as summarised by Project NOMAD (natural_remedies.json, commit 54ee30a)\nlicence: text Apache-2.0 (Project NOMAD), summarising a US government page (public domain)\norigin: nomad/natural_remedies.json#aloe-vera\n\nAloe vera gel is applied topically for burns, acne, and various skin conditions including psoriasis and radiation-related skin damage. Oral use is promoted for digestive conditions, though topical applications have more research support.\n\n## how\nApply gel from the inner leaf of the aloe plant (or a commercially prepared aloe gel) directly to the affected skin area. Topical use is the application with the most research support; oral aloe products carry more serious risks and should not be used without consulting a healthcare provider.\n\n## evidence\nResearch suggests topical aloe gel may speed burn healing and reduce burn-related pain. Two small studies indicate aloe gel, combined with other treatments, may improve acne; evidence for other skin uses is limited.\n\n## safety\nTopical use is generally well tolerated, though occasional burning or itching may occur. Oral use carries more serious risks: the latex can cause abdominal pain and diarrhea, oral extracts have been linked to cases of acute hepatitis, and animal studies associated non-decolorized extracts with gastrointestinal cancer. Likely unsafe during pregnancy; may interact with medications such as digoxin.\n\n## used for\n- Burns\n- Acne\n- Dry & chapped skin\n\n## also said\n- sunburn on my shoulders, can i put the gel from that spiky plant on it\n- burned my hand on the stove, which plant gel helps it heal faster\n- is the clear goo inside the succulent leaf good for burns\n- can i drink aloe juice for my stomach\n- drank the leaf juice, now stomach pain and diarrhea\n- is drinking aloe bad for the liver\n- pregnant, is it ok to take aloe by mouth\n- aloe and digoxin together safe?\n- cut a leaf off the plant on my windowsill, how do i use it on skin\n- plant gel to take the sting out of a burn\n- does the leaf gel really speed up burn healing\n- skin burning and itching after putting the leaf gel on\n- dry chapped hands, rub the plant gel on them?\n- skin damaged from radiotherapy, soothing gel\n- psoriasis patches, would the green leaf gel do anything\n- is the yellow sap under the leaf skin dangerous to eat\n- aloe pills cancer risk\n- gel from the leaf or juice to drink, which is safer\n- scald from boiling water, which plant to calm it\n- acne, can leaf gel help alongside my cream\n- alo vera for burnt skin\n- the cactus looking plant with gel inside, safe for skin?\n"
