   "name": "blackberry.md",
   "text": "title: Blackberry\naka: blackberries, bramble, brambles, dewberry, Brombeere, Brombeeren, mûre, mure, mûre sauvage, ronce, Rubus fruticosus, Rubus\ntags: plant wild food berry\nwarn: A blackberry is made of many small round beads packed together. A smooth, single shiny black berry is something else and may be poisonous.\nsee: deadly-lookalikes, foraging-rules\nsource: written from general botanical knowledge; verify before relying on it\nlicence: CC0 (metos)\n\nBlackberries grow on bramble, a thorny, arching shrub found almost everywhere in Central Europe. The fruit is common, easy to recognise and safe once identified, which makes it one of the most useful wild foods of late summer.\n\n## identify\n- Fruit made of many small round juicy segments packed together, green then red then glossy black when ripe.\n- When picked, the ripe fruit comes off with its white core still inside (unlike raspberry, which leaves the core on the plant).\n- Long arching or trailing stems with hooked prickles that catch on clothes.\n- Leaves split into three or five toothed leaflets, spread like a hand; often prickly along the central vein underneath.\n- White or pale pink flowers with five petals, from early summer, often on the same plant as ripe fruit later on.\n- Grows in hedges, woodland edges, clearings, waste ground and along paths.\n\n## lookalikes\nDewberry is a smaller, low-growing relative whose fruit has fewer segments and a bluish waxy coating. It is also edible.\n\nThe real danger is mixing up blackberries with single black berries. Deadly nightshade has shiny, cherry-sized black berries sitting on a green five-pointed star, on a soft, non-woody plant without thorns. It is very poisonous. Buckthorn, privet, dogwood, ivy and elder also carry black berries, but each berry is single and smooth, often in clusters, and none of them is made of packed segments. If the fruit is not made of many small beads, do not eat it.\n\n## eat\nRipe black fruit can be eaten raw, cooked into jam, sauce or compote, or dried. Red or hard fruit is unripe and sour. Wash fruit picked near the ground. Young leaves are sometimes dried for tea.\n\n## harvest\nFrom about late July to September, depending on altitude and weather. Pick only fully black, soft fruit; the best fruit is often at the tips. Avoid fruit at dog height along busy paths, near roads and on sprayed field edges. Late-season fruit that is mouldy, watery or full of insects is better left.\n\n## also said\n- blackberry\n- blackberries\n- brombeere\n- mure sauvage\n- ronce fruit edible\n- small black berries on a bush with thorns by the path\n- black berry made of little balls on a prickly plant\n- thorny bush with black fruit in the hedge can we eat it\n- brambles everywhere with dark berries are they safe\n- how to tell blackberry from deadly nightshade\n- shiny black single berries on a plant without thorns\n- when are blackberries ripe\n- red blackberries still sour can i eat them\n- blackberry or raspberry difference\n- the fruit comes off with the core inside which one is that\n- dewberry edible\n- blue coated small blackberry low on the ground\n- can i eat blackberries raw\n- blackberry jam from wild berries\n- blackberrys by the road ok to pick\n- should i wash wild blackberries\n- blackberry leaves tea\n- late blackberries in october mouldy still good\n- kids picking berries on the walk which black ones are safe\n- black berrys on long spiky stems\n"
