   "name": "blackthorn.md",
   "text": "title: Blackthorn\naka: sloe, sloes, sloe berry, blackthorn berries, Schlehe, Schlehen, Schlehdorn, Schwarzdorn, prunellier, prunelle, épine noire, Prunus spinosa\ntags: plant wild food berry\nwarn: Do not crack or eat the stones: the kernels contain substances that release cyanide. Sloes are very sour and dry the mouth raw; buckthorn has similar black berries and is a strong laxative.\nsee: deadly-lookalikes, foraging-rules\nsource: written from general botanical knowledge; verify before relying on it\nlicence: CC0 (metos)\n\nBlackthorn is a dense, very thorny shrub of hedges and woodland edges, covered in white blossom in early spring before its leaves appear. Its small blue-black fruits, sloes, are too sour to eat raw in quantity but are useful cooked, and they stay on the bush late into autumn.\n\n## identify\n- Dense, spreading shrub, usually 1 to 4 m, often forming thickets.\n- Dark, almost black bark; side shoots end in long, stiff, sharp thorns.\n- Pure white five-petalled flowers in March and April, before the leaves.\n- Small oval leaves, finely toothed, alternate.\n- Round fruit about 1 to 1.5 cm, blue-black with a pale blue waxy bloom that rubs off.\n- Each fruit holds one hard stone, like a small plum.\n- Very sour and mouth-drying taste.\n- Grows in hedges, field margins, scrub and woodland edges.\n\n## lookalikes\nBullace and wild plums are close relatives with larger fruit; they are edible and prepared the same way.\n\nBuckthorn has black berries, but they are shiny without a waxy bloom, contain several small seeds rather than one stone, and its leaves are rounder with veins curving towards the tip. Its berries are a strong laxative and should not be eaten. Privet and dogwood have black berries in clusters, without a stone. Deadly nightshade is a soft, non-woody plant with shiny black berries on a green star; it is very poisonous.\n\nA sloe is a small blue plum: a waxy blue-black skin and one stone. If there is no single stone, it is not a sloe.\n\n## eat\nRaw sloes are very astringent; a few are harmless if the stones are spat out, but they are not pleasant. Frost or a night in the freezer softens them and reduces the sourness. Cook into jam, jelly, syrup, juice or sauce, usually mixed with sweeter fruit; strain out the stones after cooking. Do not crush the stones while cooking.\n\n## harvest\nFrom about October to December, ideally after the first frosts. Wear thick gloves: blackthorn punctures often become infected, so clean any wound. Avoid roadsides and sprayed field edges.\n\n## also said\n- blackthorn\n- sloe\n- sloes\n- schlehe\n- prunelle\n- small blue black berries on a thorny bush\n- tiny plums with blue coating in the hedge\n- dark blue berries that dry out your mouth\n- can i eat sloes raw\n- are sloe stones poisonous\n- sloe jam\n- when to pick sloes after frost\n- freeze sloes before using\n- bush with white flowers in march before the leaves\n- very thorny black bush with blue berries\n- sloe or buckthorn\n- black berries without stone on a bush are they sloes\n- prunellier fruit comestible\n- schlehen kochen\n- sloe berry syrup\n- slow berries edible\n- cut my hand on blackthorn and now its swollen\n- blue fruit with one stone like a cherry\n- autumn berries with a whitish blue bloom\n- what can i make with sloes\n"
