   "name": "common-mallow.md",
   "text": "title: Common mallow\naka: mallow, wild mallow, high mallow, dwarf mallow, cheeses, Wilde Malve, Rosspappel, Weg-Malve, mauve sylvestre, grande mauve, Malva sylvestris, Malva neglecta\ntags: plant wild food\nwarn: Learn it in flower first. Before flowering, the rounded leaves can be confused with poisonous plants such as marsh marigold or lesser celandine.\nsee: deadly-lookalikes, foraging-rules\nsource: written from general botanical knowledge; verify before relying on it\nlicence: CC0 (metos)\n\nCommon mallow and its smaller relative dwarf mallow grow on paths, field edges and waste ground. Leaves, flowers and the small green seed heads are all edible, with a mild taste. Cooked leaves become slightly slimy, which thickens soups.\n\n## identify\n- Flowers have five petals, each notched at the tip. Common mallow: pink-purple petals with darker stripes, 2.5 to 4 cm wide. Dwarf mallow: smaller, pale pink to almost white.\n- Leaves are round to kidney-shaped, with five to seven shallow lobes and rounded teeth, on long stalks.\n- Veins spread out from the base of the leaf like the fingers of a hand.\n- Leaves are soft and slightly hairy, not glossy.\n- Green seed heads are flat, round discs made of segments, like a tiny wheel of cheese.\n- No strong smell. Chewed leaves feel slimy.\n- Common mallow grows upright or sprawling, 30 to 120 cm. Dwarf mallow creeps along the ground.\n- Grows in sunny, dry places: paths, walls, field edges, waste ground, near villages and farms.\n\n## lookalikes\nMarsh marigold (Caltha palustris) is poisonous. Its leaves are round or kidney-shaped but glossy and hairless, and it has large yellow flowers. It grows in wet places: streams, ditches and marshy meadows, where mallow does not.\n\nLesser celandine (Ficaria verna) is poisonous raw. Its leaves are glossy, hairless and heart-shaped, and it has yellow star-shaped flowers in early spring.\n\nCranesbills (Geranium) have rounded leaves too, but cut much more deeply, and often a strong smell. They are not dangerous.\n\nGround ivy (Glechoma) has small rounded leaves on a square stem and smells strongly when crushed. It is not dangerous.\n\nIf the leaves are glossy and hairless or the flowers are yellow, it is not mallow.\n\n## eat\n- Young leaves raw in salads, or cooked in soups and stews, where they act as a thickener.\n- Flowers raw, in salads.\n- Green seed heads (\"cheeses\") raw as a nibble, or cooked. They taste mild and nutty.\n- Older leaves are better cooked.\n- Like spinach, mallow can hold more nitrate when it grows on heavily manured ground. Eat normal vegetable portions.\n\n## harvest\n- Young leaves from April to September; flowers from June to September; seed heads while still green.\n- Pick leaves and flowers by hand and leave the plant to regrow.\n- It often grows next to paths and walls: avoid roadsides, dog-walking paths, manure heaps and sprayed field edges.\n\n## also said\n- mallow\n- common mallow\n- malve\n- wilde malve\n- mauve\n- mauve sylvestre\n- malva sylvestris edible\n- pink flowers with dark stripes and round leaves\n- purple striped flower with five notched petals\n- round leaves with shallow lobes, a bit hairy\n- little green wheels like cheese on a plant\n- can you eat mallow cheeses\n- mallow leaves slimy\n- plant to thicken soup in the wild\n- dwarf mallow\n- mallow or geranium\n- round leaves by the path, is it marsh marigold\n- mallow flowers edible\n- malow\n- wild hibiscus looking flower\n- weg malve\n- can i eat mallow raw\n- mallow seeds taste nutty\n- round leaf weed on the farm path\n- rosspappel\n"
