   "name": "feverfew.md",
   "text": "title: Feverfew\naka: Tanacetum parthenium\ntags: plant medicinal herb\nwarn: People sensitive to ragweed may experience allergic reactions. Feverfew may slow blood clotting and should be stopped at least 2 weeks before scheduled surgery. It may interact with migraine medications and should be avoided during pregnancy due to potential effects on uterine contractions.\nsource: https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/feverfew, as summarised by Project NOMAD (natural_remedies.json, commit 54ee30a)\nlicence: text Apache-2.0 (Project NOMAD), summarising a US government page (public domain)\norigin: nomad/natural_remedies.json#feverfew\n\nFeverfew is promoted for migraine headache prevention and for minor head and tension pain. Topically, it is marketed for itching and skin irritation.\n\n## evidence\nA 2020 systematic review of seven migraine studies found inconsistent results, though some evidence suggests feverfew may reduce migraine frequency and associated symptoms such as nausea and light sensitivity. There is little or no evidence supporting feverfew for other health conditions.\n\n## safety\nSide effects include nausea, digestive issues, bloating, and mouth sores from chewing fresh leaves. People sensitive to ragweed may experience allergic reactions. Feverfew may slow blood clotting and should be stopped at least 2 weeks before scheduled surgery. It may interact with migraine medications and should be avoided during pregnancy due to potential effects on uterine contractions.\n\n## used for\n- Headache\n\n## also said\n- migraine with nausea and light hurting my eyes, herb to prevent\n- chewing fresh leaves for headache gave me mouth sores\n- little daisy herb for migraine prevention\n- feverfew before surgery, when to stop\n- surgery in 10 days, i take a migraine herb, should i stop\n- feverfew and triptans together\n- pregnant, herb for migraine that affects the uterus\n- migraine herb that slows blood clotting\n- allergic to ragweed, feverfew ok?\n- fever few for headache\n- tanacetum for migraine\n- does the leaf really reduce migraine frequency\n- light sensitivity during migraines, natural preventive\n- migraine herb gives me bloating\n- fresh leaves from a garden herb for headaches\n- feverfew cream for itchy skin\n- minor tension head pain, little white flower herb\n- is the evidence for that daisy migraine herb any good\n- migraine herb that interacts with my migraine meds\n- migraine herb thins blood, stop before an operation\n- nauseous migraines, garden plant leaves to prevent them\n"
