   "name": "ginger.md",
   "text": "title: Ginger\naka: Zingiber officinale\ntags: plant medicinal herb\nwarn: Possible interactions with medications exist; consult a healthcare provider before use, especially during pregnancy or breastfeeding.\nsource: https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ginger, as summarised by Project NOMAD (natural_remedies.json, commit 54ee30a)\nlicence: text Apache-2.0 (Project NOMAD), summarising a US government page (public domain)\norigin: nomad/natural_remedies.json#ginger\n\nGinger is traditionally and commonly used for nausea and vomiting, indigestion, menstrual cramps, and osteoarthritis.\n\n## evidence\nResearch shows ginger may be helpful for nausea and vomiting associated with pregnancy. Studies suggest ginger dietary supplements might be helpful for reducing the severity of menstrual cramps and for knee osteoarthritis symptoms; effectiveness for chemotherapy- or post-surgery-related nausea remains uncertain.\n\n## safety\nSide effects when taken orally include abdominal discomfort, heartburn, diarrhea, and mouth and throat irritation. Possible interactions with medications exist; consult a healthcare provider before use, especially during pregnancy or breastfeeding.\n\n## used for\n- Nausea & vomiting\n- Menstrual cramps\n- Indigestion & upset stomach\n\n## also said\n- morning sickness, what can i take naturally\n- pregnant and throwing up every morning\n- nausea in pregnancy, root tea\n- period cramps, root to help\n- painful menstrual cramps natural remedy\n- ginger for period pain does it work\n- car sick, chew some ginger?\n- feeling queasy, spicy root tea\n- root tea for vomiting\n- spicy root tea gives me heartburn\n- ginger with blood thinners\n- knee osteoarthritis ginger supplement\n- ginger for nausea after chemo\n- sick after surgery, spicy root tea help?\n- is the root for nausea safe while breastfeeding\n- spicy root for upset tummy\n- spicy root burns my mouth and throat\n- zingiber for nausea\n- how much of the root is safe in pregnancy for nausea\n- gingr tea indigestion\n- seasick, root to chew\n- nausea capsules from a root gave me diarrhea\n"
