   "name": "lime-tree.md",
   "text": "title: Lime tree\naka: linden, linden tree, small-leaved lime, large-leaved lime, common lime, basswood, Linde, Winterlinde, Sommerlinde, Lindenblüten, tilleul, Tilia cordata, Tilia platyphyllos, Tilia × europaea\ntags: plant wild food tree\nwarn: Lime tree here means the linden, not the citrus fruit. Only the young, soft spring leaves are good to eat.\nsee: deadly-lookalikes, foraging-rules\nsource: written from general botanical knowledge; verify before relying on it\nlicence: CC0 (metos)\n\nA large tree with heart-shaped leaves, planted in villages, avenues and parks and also wild in mixed woodland. Its young leaves are a mild salad green in spring, and its flowers make a well-known calming tea. Shoots growing from the base of the trunk put fresh leaves within easy reach.\n\n## identify\n- Flowers in summer: small, creamy-yellow, strongly sweet-scented, in hanging clusters attached to a long, pale green, tongue-shaped leafy wing (bract). Bees hum in the tree.\n- Fruit: small round hard nutlets, still hanging from the same pale wing into autumn.\n- Leaves heart-shaped with a pointed tip, finely toothed, and lopsided at the base (one side lower than the other). They alternate along the twig.\n- Small-leaved lime has tufts of rusty-brown hairs where the veins meet on the leaf underside. Large-leaved lime has bigger, softer, hairy leaves.\n- Many trees have a dense cluster of shoots around the base of the trunk.\n- Bark grey, smooth on young trees, later with shallow ridges.\n- Flowers June to July.\n\n## lookalikes\n- Hazel leaves are rounder, double-toothed and roughly hairy, and hazel is a many-stemmed shrub with nuts. See hazel.\n- Lilac has heart-shaped leaves too, but they are smooth-edged with no teeth and grow in opposite pairs.\n- Indian bean tree (catalpa), planted in parks, has very large heart-shaped leaves with no teeth, often in groups of three, and long thin bean pods. Do not eat it.\n- Birch leaves are smaller and triangular, on a tree with white bark.\n- The key checks for lime: toothed edge, lopsided base, alternate leaves, and the pale winged bract with flowers or nutlets.\n\n## eat\n- Young leaves raw in salad or as a sandwich green. They are mild and slightly slimy in a pleasant way. They become tough in early summer.\n- Flowers, picked with their pale bract, dried in the shade, make tea: a spoonful steeped in hot water for several minutes. It is traditionally used to calm and to help sleep. Drink in moderation.\n- Fresh flowers can be eaten in small amounts.\n- Leaves can be eaten in meal-sized amounts as a salad green.\n\n## harvest\n- Leaves: April to May, while bright green, thin and soft. Basal shoots and low branches keep producing soft leaves a little longer.\n- Flowers: June to July, when most flowers on a cluster have just opened. Dry them quickly in an airy, shaded place and store dry.\n- Avoid trees along busy roads and car parks. Aphids often cover the leaves in sticky honeydew in summer; wash or choose clean leaves.\n\n## also said\n- lime tree\n- linden\n- linden leaves edible\n- linde\n- lindenblütentee\n- tilleul\n- tisane de tilleul\n- tree with heart shaped leaves that are lopsided at the base\n- heart shaped leaf with small teeth can i eat it\n- sweet smelling tree with little yellow flowers in june\n- flowers hanging from a long pale leaf\n- tree full of bees in summer smells of honey\n- young lime leaves salad\n- can you eat lime tree leaves\n- lime flower tea how to make\n- tea to help sleep from a tree\n- lime tree not the fruit\n- lindon tree\n- little round seeds on a wing hanging from the tree\n- shoots at bottom of trunk with soft leaves\n- basswood edible\n- when to pick linden flowers\n- drying lime flowers\n- sticky leaves under the tree\n- wild salad leaves from a tree in spring\n"
