   "name": "raspberry.md",
   "text": "title: Raspberry\naka: raspberries, wild raspberry, Himbeere, Himbeeren, Waldhimbeere, framboise, framboisier, framboise sauvage, Rubus idaeus\ntags: plant wild food berry\nwarn: A raspberry is a red fruit made of many small beads that pulls off its core and is hollow inside. Smooth single red berries on other shrubs are often poisonous.\nsee: deadly-lookalikes, foraging-rules\nsource: written from general botanical knowledge; verify before relying on it\nlicence: CC0 (metos)\n\nThe wild raspberry is the same plant as the garden raspberry, with smaller and often sweeter fruit. It grows in clearings, woodland edges and mountain slopes across Central Europe and is one of the easiest wild fruits to recognise.\n\n## identify\n- Red fruit made of many small juicy segments, slightly soft and hairy.\n- The ripe fruit pulls off easily and leaves a white cone on the plant, so the fruit is hollow like a thimble.\n- Upright, mostly unbranched canes up to about 2 m, with many fine, soft prickles rather than hooked thorns.\n- Leaves split into three to seven toothed leaflets; the underside is clearly white or silvery.\n- Small white flowers that hang down, in early summer.\n- Grows in forest clearings, cut or burnt areas, woodland edges, often in large patches.\n\n## lookalikes\nUnripe blackberries are red too, but they are hard, sour and do not come off their core. Wait until they turn black.\n\nSeveral poisonous shrubs in Central Europe have red berries: fly honeysuckle (red berries in pairs), mezereon (red berries stuck directly to the stem), guelder rose (shiny, see-through red berries in flat clusters), yew (a red cup around a dark seed), bryony (red berries on a climbing vine) and spindle (pink four-lobed capsules with orange seeds). None of them has a fruit made of packed beads that is hollow inside. If it is not hollow, do not eat it.\n\n## eat\nRipe fruit is good raw, or as jam, syrup, sauce or dried fruit. Young leaves are dried for tea. Wash fruit, especially from low canes.\n\nIn parts of Central Europe the fox tapeworm is present, and some people cook or wash low-growing wild berries for that reason. The risk from berries is thought to be low but not zero; washing reduces it, cooking removes it, normal freezing does not.\n\n## harvest\nFrom about July to August, later in the mountains. Pick when deep red and the fruit slips off easily. Pick into a flat container, as the fruit crushes easily. Avoid roadsides and sprayed field edges.\n\n## also said\n- raspberry\n- raspberries\n- himbeere\n- framboise sauvage\n- wild raspberries in the forest\n- red berry that is hollow inside like a thimble\n- red berries on canes with white under the leaves\n- red fruit made of little balls in a clearing\n- is this a raspberry or something poisonous\n- small red berries on a bush can i eat them\n- red berries in pairs on a shrub are they raspberries\n- raspberry or unripe blackberry\n- when are wild raspberries ripe\n- can i eat wild raspberries raw\n- raspberry leaf tea\n- framboisier sauvage feuilles\n- fox tapeworm raspberries\n- do i need to wash wild raspberries\n- rasberry wild edible\n- raspberry jam from wild fruit\n- raspberries in the mountains in august\n- red berries near the trail, my kid wants to eat them\n- prickly canes with soft red berries\n- difference between raspberry and blackberry plant\n- the berry comes off and leaves a white cone\n"
