   "name": "sweet-chestnut.md",
   "text": "title: Sweet chestnut\naka: chestnut, chestnuts, Spanish chestnut, Edelkastanie, Esskastanie, Marone, Maroni, châtaignier, châtaigne, marron, Castanea sativa\ntags: plant wild food nut tree\nwarn: Horse chestnut conkers are toxic and look similar. Sweet chestnuts have a pointed tip with a little tuft and grow in a husk of dense, needle-sharp spines; conkers are round with no tip.\nsee: deadly-lookalikes, foraging-rules\nsource: written from general botanical knowledge; verify before relying on it\nlicence: CC0 (metos)\n\nA large tree whose nuts are starchy and filling, and can be roasted, boiled, dried or ground into flour. It grows in warmer, lime-poor areas: much of France, southern Switzerland, the wine-growing parts of south-west Germany, and a few warm parts of Austria. It is also planted in parks.\n\n## identify\n- Husk green, turning yellow-brown, densely covered in long, thin, sharp spines, like a hedgehog. It hurts to hold.\n- Usually two or three nuts per husk, glossy brown, flat on one side where they pressed together, ending in a pointed tip with a small pale tuft.\n- Leaves long and narrow, about 15 to 25 cm, single (not in a hand-shaped group), with sharp, bristle-tipped teeth along the edge.\n- Bark on older trees has deep ridges that often twist in a spiral up the trunk.\n- Long creamy-yellow flower catkins in June and July, with a heavy smell.\n- Nuts fall from late September to November.\n\n## lookalikes\nHorse chestnut (conker tree; Rosskastanie, marronnier d'Inde) is toxic and very common in towns, parks and avenues. Eating conkers causes vomiting, stomach pain and diarrhoea, and can be more serious.\n\n- Leaves: horse chestnut has large hand-shaped leaves of five to seven leaflets spreading from one point. Sweet chestnut leaves are single.\n- Husk: horse chestnut has a thick, fleshy husk with a few short, stubby spikes, or none. Sweet chestnut husks are covered all over in fine, dense spines.\n- Nut: a conker is usually one per husk, large, round and shiny, with a big pale scar and no point or tuft. A sweet chestnut is flatter on one side and ends in a pointed tip with a tuft.\n- Flowers: horse chestnut has upright white or pink \"candles\" in May.\n\nIn French, \"marron\" is used for both: sweet chestnuts are sold as marrons, while the horse chestnut tree is the marronnier d'Inde. Go by the features, not the name.\n\nBeech nuts are much smaller and triangular. See beech.\n\n## eat\n- Always cook them. Raw sweet chestnuts are edible in small amounts but are astringent and hard to digest.\n- Before roasting, cut a cross or a slit through the shell of each nut, or they can burst.\n- Roast in embers, in a pan with holes, or in a hot oven, for roughly 20 to 30 minutes until the inside is soft. Peel while warm, removing the shell and the brown inner skin.\n- Or boil scored nuts in water for about 20 to 30 minutes and peel.\n- Dried, peeled chestnuts keep for months and can be ground into flour for flatbread, porridge or soup.\n- They are mostly starch, so they can be eaten in meal-sized amounts like potatoes.\n\n## harvest\n- Late September to November. Collect nuts from the ground after they fall.\n- Wear sturdy shoes and gloves. Opening a husk by pressing it with both boots works well.\n- Discard nuts with small holes (insect larvae), mould, or a hollow rattle. Nuts that float in water are often bad.\n- Fresh chestnuts spoil within days to weeks; dry or cook them to keep.\n- Avoid trees along busy roads. Check the land rules: in some areas chestnut groves are private and gathering is restricted.\n\n## also said\n- sweet chestnut\n- chestnuts wild\n- edelkastanie\n- maroni\n- châtaigne\n- spiky green husk with brown nuts inside under a big tree\n- is this a conker or a chestnut i can eat\n- horse chestnut or sweet chestnut how to tell\n- conkers edible?\n- my kid ate a conker\n- chestnut with pointy tip and hairy tuft\n- husk like a hedgehog with 3 nuts\n- long leaves with sharp teeth spiky balls on the ground\n- how to roast chestnuts on fire\n- why do chestnuts explode\n- cut a cross in chestnuts\n- boil chestnuts how long\n- can you eat chestnuts raw\n- chesnut flour\n- dry chestnuts for winter\n- marron or chataigne difference\n- rosskastanie giftig\n- kastanien essen oder giftig\n- chestnuts with little holes in them\n- how to open spiky chestnut husk without hurting hands\n"
